Chronic Inflammatory Response Syndrome (CIRS) / Biotoxin Illness
Integrative & Functional medicine
Exposure to water-damaged buildings, Severe brain fog/fatigue, Migrating severe joint pain, Reduced visual contrast sensitivity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IFM-005
Reason for Visit: Acute presentation of severe, unprovoked blurred vision, intense migrating joint pain, and neuropathic distress requiring urgent assessment to rule out acute demyelinating disease or acute closed-angle glaucoma.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute onset of blurred vision, severe 'ice-pick' headaches, and severe neuropathic pain. Urgent after-hours physical and neurological assessment was strictly required to exclude acute optic neuritis, acute glaucoma, or a cerebrovascular event, preventing irreversible neurological or visual deterioration prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My vision suddenly went blurry an hour ago, I have an ice-pick stabbing behind my eye, and my arms and legs are burning."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a sudden worsening of chronic symptoms, presenting tonight with sudden, unprovoked blurred vision and a distinct 'ice-pick' severe headache. This acute episode is superimposed on a 6-month history of profound, debilitating fatigue and severe post-exertional malaise. The patient describes migrating, severe sharp joint pain without noticeable swelling, and numbness with intense tingling and generalized neuropathic pain in a non-dermatomal distribution across all four limbs. The patient reports severe, unremitting brain fog and poor concentration. The patient notes a distinct history of prolonged residential exposure to a severe water-damaged building with visible indoor mold prior to moving to the current residence. Additionally, the patient reports chronic sinus congestion, a prominent metallic taste, and frequent distinct 'static shocks'.
Functional Status: Currently unable to read or safely ambulate due to visual disturbances and severe neuropathic pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to drive or safely navigate the home due to visual impairment.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/90 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Patient appears highly distressed, rubbing the temples and squinting aggressively.
Physical Exam: Eye examination reveals equal, round, and reactive pupils; extraocular movements are intact. Visual acuity is subjectively reduced, but confrontational visual fields are full. Fundoscopy reveals sharp optic discs with no papilledema, and globes are soft to palpation (ruling out gross acute glaucoma). Neurological exam shows intact cranial nerves, but profound difficulty assimilating new information and following complex commands. Sensation to light touch elicits generalized allodynia in a non-dermatomal pattern. Musculoskeletal exam shows no focal joint swelling or intense erythema despite severe subjective pain. Clinical findings correlate with severe brain fog, fatigue, and migrating severe joint pain. Future specialized blood panels (C4a, TGF-beta-1) and visual contrast sensitivity testing (VCS) are anticipated to assist in confirming the diagnosis.
Environmental Observations: Current home appears clean, dry, and free of visible mold or musty odors. No obvious trip hazards.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking over-the-counter antihistamines; no discrepancies noted.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Inflammatory Response Syndrome (CIRS) / Biotoxin Illness - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Optic Neuritis (Multiple Sclerosis presentation) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral pupillary reactivity without an afferent pupillary defect (Marcus Gunn pupil) and normal color vision on crude testing.

Acute Closed-Angle Glaucoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of corneal clouding, mid-dilated fixed pupil, or palpably stony-hard globes.

Acute Giant Cell Arteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Age profile (if younger), absence of palpable, thickened, or tender temporal arteries, and no jaw claudication.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute onset blurred vision, severe focal headache, and diffuse neuropathic pain. Telephone triage rightly flagged the emergent risk of acute demyelinating pathology (optic neuritis), acute glaucoma, or a cerebrovascular event. Immediate on-site clinical assessment was strictly necessary to perform a targeted ophthalmologic and neurological exam to preserve vision and rule out central nervous system ischemia. The exclusion of acute intraocular pressure emergencies and focal gross neurological deficits confirmed immediate safety, but the severity of the sensory distortions and pain dictated that delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed an unacceptable risk of profound psychological distress, falls, and diagnostic delay of a potential evolving central lesion. This encounter satisfies the strict criteria for urgent after-hours assessment (MBS Items 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the absence of acute vision-threatening emergencies. Administered simple analgesia (Paracetamol/Ibuprofen combination) for the severe headache. Recommend formal optometry review and a comprehensive biotoxin panel (C4a, TGF-beta-1, MSH) via routine GP.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the profound neuropathic and inflammatory flares, initiate strict environmental and dietary controls. Action: Utilize a HEPA air purifier in the bedroom running 24/7. Consequence: Continuous filtration of ambient biotoxins reduces the immediate inflammatory load, rewarding the patient with a decrease in chronic sinus congestion and brain fog upon waking. Action: Drink 500ml of filtered water immediately upon experiencing 'static shocks'. Consequence: The static shocks reflect altered cellular osmolality; rapid hydration corrects this imbalance, directly eliminating the uncomfortable electrical sensations.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated currently.
DME/Safety (Optional): HEPA air filtration unit recommended for the primary living space.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 72 hours for specialist referral (Ophthalmology/Immunology) and comprehensive blood panels.

